## Context

This rule ports chess.com's structural design taste (spacing, radius, shadow, component reuse,
restraint) onto Slope Battles. It does NOT port chess.com's colors. Slope Battles' existing dark
neon-alpine palette is a hard constraint — treat every color value below as fixed, not a starting
point. The goal is a chess.com-caliber layout/interaction system wearing Slope Battles' own colors,
with a freestyle-ski sensibility (fast, physical, competitive — not corporate).

## Design Tokens — from chess.com (apply these; colors excluded)

**Spacing** — 4px base unit. Scale: 2, 4, 8 (dominant), 12, 16, 24, 40, 80, 96px.
**Radius** — 3px (chips) / 5px (buttons, small tiles) / 10px (feature cards). Child radius ≤ parent.
**Shadow** — 3-layer construction on every elevated surface, never flat:
`inset 0 1px 0 <highlight>, 0 1px 2px <shadow-a>, 0 2px 4px <shadow-b>`
(build this with Slope Battles' own `--shadow` / `--glow-ice` / `--glow-fire` tokens, not chess.com's rgba values)
**Grid** — 1084px-equivalent max-width content container, 16px side padding. Dense multi-column grids for card collections (4-col @ ~242px, 16/12px gutter) rather than single-column stacks.
**Typography rhythm** — tight practical scale, not a strict geometric ratio: hero ~42px/800, section heading ~36px/800, UI title 14px/600, body 14-16px/400, micro-label 12px/600. Keep Slope Battles' existing font families (Inter / Bebas Neue / Barlow Condensed) mapped onto these roles — Bebas Neue or Barlow Condensed 800 for hero/headline weight (chess.com's "Chess Sans" role), Inter for UI/body (chess.com's "-apple-system" role).
**Motion** — only `transform` / `opacity` / `background-color`. 0.05s for hover/press feedback, 0.3s `cubic-bezier(0.2,1,0.3,1)` for expand/collapse. Never animate `width`/`height`/`top`/`left`.

## Taste Directives (imperative)

1. When designing any hero/landing surface, always show the actual product (a live duel board, a real leaderboard row, a real rating chart) instead of illustrated or stock-photo hero art — because credibility comes from showing the real interface, not a mood.
2. When placing a color, always ask "is this the one action I want clicked?" — reserve `--primary` (ice) for the single primary CTA per screen (Challenge, Start Duel, Confirm) and keep every other surface neutral (bg/text/border tokens only) — because color that always means "click here" removes decoding work from the user.
3. When building any elevated surface (card, button, modal), always give it a 3-layer shadow (inset highlight + 2 stacked drops) built from Slope Battles' existing shadow/glow tokens — because on a dark background a flat surface is invisible without shadow doing the lift.
4. When adding a new content area (leaderboard entry, trick card, friend row, tournament tile), always reuse the existing icon-tile component shape (icon/avatar + bold title + one muted description line, same radius/padding) rather than inventing a new layout — because one learned pattern lets users scan the whole app the same way as features grow.
5. When sizing radius, always scale it with the component: 5px for buttons/small chips, 10px for cards, nested children get equal-or-smaller radius than their parent — never larger.
6. When aligning text, default to left-aligned for headlines, card content, and hero copy — avoid centering as the default, even for hero moments — because it reads as "app," not "landing page."
7. When adding motion, keep it to fast (0.05-0.3s) transform/opacity/background-color transitions with an ease-out-snap curve — respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
8. Freestyle-ski specificity: where chess.com uses chess iconography (piece silhouettes, board squares) as its native texture, Slope Battles should lean on its own native texture — trick names, grab names, spin/rotation badges, mountain/run iconography, halogen-lit night-session mood already implied by the `--halogen` token — not chess.com's literal imagery.

## Anti-patterns (Never / Avoid)

- **Never change any hex/rgba color value** in `:root` or `body.light` (`--bg-base`, `--bg-surface`, `--bg-elevated`, `--bg-card`, `--border`, `--halogen`, `--ice`, `--ice-dark`, `--ice-glow`, `--fire`, `--fire-dark`, `--fire-glow`, `--success`, `--danger`, `--warning`, `--text-primary`, `--text-secondary`, `--text-muted`) or their light-mode overrides. Restructure how they're used; do not redefine what they equal.
- Never introduce a new accent color outside the existing `--primary` (ice) / `--accent` (fire) pair — chess.com's single-accent restraint applies to Slope Battles' existing two-accent system, not as license to add a third.
- Avoid flat, shadowless cards/buttons on the dark surfaces — every elevated element needs the 3-layer shadow treatment.
- Avoid centering hero/marketing copy — left-align by default.
- Avoid one-off bespoke layouts per feature (a unique leaderboard layout, a unique friends-list layout, a unique tournament-card layout) — converge on the shared icon-tile shape instead.
- Avoid animating layout properties (`width`, `height`, `top`, `left`, `margin`) — transform/opacity only.
- Avoid arbitrary spacing values outside the 4px scale (2/4/8/12/16/24/40/80/96) — no 15px, 18px, 22px gaps.
- Avoid literal chess.com imagery or terminology — this is a reference for structure/rhythm/restraint, not a skin to copy verbatim.
